cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Twenty Firmware Projects on the NUCLEO-H7A3ZI-Q"
message: "If you refer to this work, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Pagaran
    given-names: Joseph Ambrose
    orcid: ""
abstract: >-
  Twenty self-contained firmware projects on one STM32H7A3 board, built from a
  fixed bench inventory with no oscilloscope and no logic analyser, so every
  timing and energy claim comes from the board's own counters or from a named
  instrument. Covers a cross toolchain and linker script owned end to end,
  lock-free buffering and memory ordering on the Cortex-M7, interrupt and
  transfer-driven receive, framing with the hardware CRC unit, low-power modes
  with a measured charge per cycle, a payload codec with a host twin, three
  sensor shields and their bus topologies, the placement question for a
  classifier, the vendor abstraction layer against the registers, transforms
  with a numerical acceptance test, the cache and memory protection trap this
  family is known for, and a real-time kernel application with a measured
  latency distribution. Written against this part's own reference manual rather
  than the better known member of its family, whose documentation most published
  material assumes.
keywords:
  - embedded firmware
  - STM32H7A3
  - Cortex-M7
  - low power
  - DMA
  - cache coherency
  - FreeRTOS
  - sensor fusion
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/ambrosiobing/firmware-nucleo-h7"
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-09-29"
